  "strategy": "1. Approach and Rationale\nThis approach combines a parametric basis representation with adaptive optimization. The central idea is to use few, carefully designed basis functions to parameterize the sequence, reducing the optimization space while maintaining expressive power. The basis functions will be constructed using orthogonal polynomials and exponential decay functions, which demonstrate favorable autocorrelation properties. By optimizing the basis parameters and coefficients jointly, we aim to find sequences that minimize the AC1 upper bound. This method is inspired by theoretical results in signal processing that suggest sequences minimizing the autocorrelation bound often exhibit exponential decay and structured spectral properties.\n\n2. Mathematical Specification\nThe sequence is represented as a linear combination of K basis functions:\n    x(t) = \u03a3_{k=1}^K c_k * \u03c6_k(t)\n\nThe basis functions are generated as autoregressive filters:\n    \u03c6_k(t) = e^{-\u03b3_k^{\\prime} t} * p_k(t)\nwhere p_k(t) are orthogonal polynomials (Legendre) of degree m_k adjusted for the interval [0,n-1] and normalized to have sum 1.\n\nThe joint optimization minimizes:\n    min_{\u03b3,c} max_{\u03c4} [ (x * x)[\u03c4] / (\u2211_t x(t))^2 ] * 2*n*s\nwhere s is a scale factor to normalize the sequence energy.\n\nThe basis parameters \u03b3_k include decay rates \u03bc_k, correlations \u03c1_k, and polynomial orders m_k.\n\n3. Implementation Procedure\n- Step 1: Initialize K=5 basis functions with random parameters \u03bc in [0.5,1.0], \u03c1 in [0.8,1.0], and m in [0,2]\n- Step 2: Compute basis functions using closed-form expressions for orthogonal polynomials\n- Step 3: Use alternating optimization:\n    - Fix basis structure and optimize coefficients c to minimize initial AC1 value\n    - Fix coefficients and optimize basis parameters \u03b3 to maximize sequence convergence\n- Step 4: Apply this alternation for 500 iterations or until relative improvement < 1e-4\n- Step 5: Check and return best found candidate\n\n4. Search Choices\n- Mersenne Twister for random parameters in valid ranges\n- Polynomial order constraint: m_k \u2208 {0,1,2}\n- Multi-start from several basis families (random, default, adapted to parent sequence)\n- Gradient computation using fourier transform properties to handle the convolution efficiently\n- Communication protocol between optimization and adaptive basis sizing\n\n5. Budget and Robustness\n- Estimate time per iteration: ~0.05 seconds for small basis sets\n- Dominant costs: FFT operations and singular value decomposition in the basis matrix generation\n- Should complete 500 iterations comfortably within 1300 seconds\n- Early termination if consecutive 100 iterations show no improvement\n- Graceful degradation: switch to dense method if basis dimension becomes excessive\n\n6. Validation and Return\n- Maintain a validated best candidate throughout optimization\n- Store the exact sequence with its AC1 value\n- Evaluate final candidate against the authoritative evaluator\n- Return sequence as list of float coefficients passing all evaluator checks\n- Fallback procedure if optimization fails: return Slepian sequence project as backup",
